<h2>A complementary supply relationship, not a dependency</h2>
<p>Vietnam is India's second-largest groundnut kernel market — roughly USD 125 million in 2024 — but the character of the trade is different from Indonesia's. Vietnam grows a meaningful groundnut crop of its own, so Indian supply typically fills a specific gap: a price advantage, a volume a Vietnamese buyer can't source domestically in a given window, or timing around Vietnam's own harvest cycle. It's worth understanding that context if you're deciding how much of your sourcing to place with Indian origin versus domestic Vietnamese supply.</p>

<h2>Lighter documentation than most of Southeast Asia</h2>
<p>Vietnam doesn't carry the same regulatory layer as Indonesia — no BPOM-equivalent food registration, and halal certification is far less commonly a factor. That makes the documentation package genuinely simpler: phytosanitary certificate, certificate of origin and a certificate of analysis covering moisture, aflatoxin and foreign matter cover most orders. Confirm your specific importer's requirements regardless, since individual buyers can still have their own quality-documentation preferences.</p>

<h2>Food or oil use — tell us which</h2>
<p>As with most Southeast Asian groundnut demand, Vietnamese buyers split between direct food use and oil extraction. The grade and count specification that suits one doesn't necessarily suit the other, so flag your end use at enquiry stage.</p>

<h2>Logistics and terms</h2>
<p>Ho Chi Minh City (Cat Lai or Cai Mep terminals) is the more common routing at 10–16 days; Haiphong in the north runs slightly longer at 12–18 days. USD invoicing is standard, with both TT and LC used depending on buyer size and relationship history.</p>

